        "math_academy_id": "q-11932",
        "problem": "Given that $g(x)=ln\u2061{x}^{2}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we express $g(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=ln\u2061u,\\,u={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}}{x\\cdot \\cancel{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
